---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import BackupCalculator from '../../components/BackupCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Database Backup Size & Time Calculator" description="Estimate compressed backup size, upload time, and total storage needed for your retention policy.">
  <BackupCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why backup planning needs real numbers</h2>
  <p>A backup strategy that looked fine at launch can silently become a problem as your database grows — backup windows lengthen, storage costs climb, and upload time can exceed your maintenance window if nobody re-checks the math.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your current database size and expected compression ratio.</li>
    <li>Enter your upload bandwidth and retention period.</li>
    <li>See estimated backup size, upload time, and total storage needed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A 50GB database with 3:1 compression produces roughly a 16.7GB backup — at 100Mbps upload, that takes about 22 minutes to transfer, and 30 days of daily retention needs roughly 500GB of backup storage.</p>

  <h2>Common Backup Planning Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming compression ratio stays constant as data grows</strong> — ratios can shift as your data's composition changes (e.g. more binary blobs vs. text).</li>
    <li><strong>Not testing restore time, only backup time.</strong> A fast backup with a slow, untested restore process can turn an incident into a much longer outage than expected.</li>
    <li><strong>Underestimating total retention storage</strong> when using daily full backups instead of incremental ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Backup Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Planning storage budget for a new backup retention policy</li>
    <li>Checking if your backup window fits within a maintenance period</li>
    <li>Estimating cloud storage costs for backup retention</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Database Backup FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this account for incremental backups?</strong> This estimates full backup size — incremental/differential backup strategies typically require significantly less storage per backup after the first full one.</p>
</ToolLayout>
